\section{Generate State Transitions, Not Cycle Counts}

A functional instruction-set simulator (ISS) must reproduce what instructions do to architectural state. It does not estimate how many cycles a cache access or a pipeline dependency costs. In the February 2024 VADL preprint, arXiv:2402.09087v1, Section~4.6 generates a functional interpreter from VIR, and Sections~3.1 and~4.8 distinguish it from the cycle-accurate path, which needs microarchitecture information \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}. Two pipelines with the same architectural contract should agree on an instruction-level state trace under the same inputs even if their cycle traces do not agree.

Listing~27 gives this VADL body, with normalized spacing. It assumes the surrounding format and resources:

\begin{verbatim}
instruction ADDI : Itype = {
  X(rd) := ((X(rs1) as SInt) + immS) as Bits
}
\end{verbatim}

The generated handler has to extract both register indices, recover the signed immediate, read the old source, perform a fixed-width addition, and update the destination and PC. Reads conceptually precede writes for the instruction: this matters whenever a destination can alias a source. The listing paired with it in Section~4.6 shows simplified generated C++ with a function returning \texttt{PC + 4}. That illustration is not a portable recipe for host-language casts. An implementation needs to preserve the guest's fixed-width behavior irrespective of host signed-overflow or shift rules. It must also respect hardwired register cells from the resource definition even when they do not appear specially in the instruction body.

\section{Cache a Dispatch Target, Not the Result of an Instruction}

Instead of JIT compilation, the paper uses an interpreter with direct-threaded dispatch. A sparse hash table associates guest instruction addresses with a dispatch target and the instruction word. Very large guest address spaces do not require a same-sized dense array of host code pointers. On a miss the interpreter decodes a word using the VIR decoder and creates an entry. For complex operands, the generator can predecode fields and retain the resulting data with the entry rather than repeating all extraction each time. The register value used by the instruction is still read on each execution.

The paper describes comparing the cached word with the data-memory instruction when supporting self-modifying code. Another design it mentions is to invalidate entries at writes. The choice changes where the cost is paid. Whatever the mechanism, reuse is valid only while the instruction representation remains applicable. \Cref{fig:visual-330} diagrams the checked path; a production implementation also needs to obey the guest's rules for when a change to instruction memory becomes visible.

