\section{Replay the Same PC with a Different Word}

In this teaching test, a harness resets the PC and source register before each step and makes any code modification visible before fetching. The dispatch table is deliberately not reset.

\begin{verbatim}
step A: PC=0x100; X(5)=41; word=0x00128293
  miss -> install ADDI with rd=5, rs1=5, immS=1
  read old X(5), write 42, return PC=0x104

step B: PC=0x100; X(5)=41; word=0x00228293
  entry exists but word changed -> refresh with immS=2
  read old X(5), write 43, return PC=0x104
\end{verbatim}

A cache keyed only by PC that reuses the old word or predecoded operands could produce 42 again even though the operation class and destination have not changed. Compare results against a reference path with no cached operands. For a new signed operation test negative inputs and wraparound; for an altered PC rule check an explicit branch as well as fall-through. Section~4.6 describes tracing and co-simulation. A trace of the first divergent PC, changed register, or memory effect is more useful for diagnosing a fault than a suite's final return code alone.

It is also worth replaying step B with unchanged bytes and a different source value. The cache should be reusable, but the answer must change. These two tests distinguish stale decoded data from an accidental cache of execution results: instruction identity and operand values obtained from state have different lifetimes. For instructions that can trap, the cached handler must also recreate the same architectural fault before publishing partial effects; caching a dispatch target must not turn an exceptional instruction into a cached result.

\section{Distinguish the Measured Interpreter from Other Modes}

The user-mode emulation described in Section~3.6 supplies explicit guest-to-host syscall mappings. It does not make the functional ISA model a full-system emulator. Section~5.6 measures the generated ISS on RV32IM and the AArch64 integer subset, with software floating point in the benchmark binaries. In the reported RV32IM Embench comparison it ran more than 21 times slower than QEMU's JIT configuration and about 75 percent slower than Spike, but faster than the other QEMU configurations tested. These are results for particular workloads and prototypes. Predecoding or better host C++ code can reduce execution cost without altering the guest state transition; neither the reported times nor those optimization opportunities indicate current tool support.
